### What is the core problem?

Generating the recipient list (100 million rows) and delivering to the push provider are two different workloads at different rates. If you do them in one synchronous flow, generation overwhelms delivery. A durable queue between them converts the spiky process into a backpressured pipeline.

```java
package com.bank.notification.model;

public class NotificationJob {
    private String notificationId; // unique ID for idempotency
    private String deviceToken;
    private String title;
    private String body;
    private int priority;          // 1=critical, 2=marketing
    private long expiresAt;

    public NotificationJob(String notificationId, String deviceToken,
                            String title, String body, int priority) {
        this.notificationId = notificationId;
        this.deviceToken = deviceToken;
        this.title = title;
        this.body = body;
        this.priority = priority;
        this.expiresAt = System.currentTimeMillis() + 3600_000; // 1 hour
    }

    public String getNotificationId() { return notificationId; }
    public String getDeviceToken() { return deviceToken; }
    public boolean isExpired() { return System.currentTimeMillis() > expiresAt; }
}
```

### How does the generator produce jobs progressively?

Paginate the recipient set in chunks and enqueue a manageable number at a time. This keeps the queue bounded and lets you pause, resume, or cancel mid-flight.

```java
package com.bank.notification.generator;

import java.util.List;

public class CampaignGenerator {

    private final RecipientRepository recipientRepo;
    private final NotificationQueue queue;
    private static final int CHUNK_SIZE = 10_000;

    public CampaignGenerator(RecipientRepository recipientRepo,
                              NotificationQueue queue) {
        this.recipientRepo = recipientRepo;
        this.queue = queue;
    }

    public void generateCampaign(String campaignId, String title, String body) {
        long offset = 0;
        List<Recipient> chunk;
        do {
            chunk = recipientRepo.findChunk(offset, CHUNK_SIZE);
            for (Recipient r : chunk) {
                String notifId = campaignId + "-" + r.getUserId();
                queue.enqueue(new NotificationJob(
                    notifId, r.getDeviceToken(), title, body, 2));
            }
            offset += CHUNK_SIZE;
            // Pace: wait if queue depth exceeds threshold
        } while (chunk.size() == CHUNK_SIZE);
    }
}
```

### How do workers drain the queue with rate limiting?

Workers consume from the queue at whatever rate is sustainable. A token bucket on the outbound path ensures the fleet never exceeds the provider's limit.

```java
package com.bank.notification.worker;

import com.bank.notification.model.NotificationJob;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class DeliveryWorker implements Runnable {

    private final NotificationQueue queue;
    private final FirebaseClient firebaseClient;
    private final IdempotencyStore idempotencyStore;
    private final TokenBucket rateLimiter;
    private static final int BATCH_SIZE = 500;

    public DeliveryWorker(NotificationQueue queue, FirebaseClient firebaseClient,
                           IdempotencyStore idempotencyStore, TokenBucket rateLimiter) {
        this.queue = queue;
        this.firebaseClient = firebaseClient;
        this.idempotencyStore = idempotencyStore;
        this.rateLimiter = rateLimiter;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        while (true) {
            List<NotificationJob> batch = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int i = 0; i < BATCH_SIZE; i++) {
                NotificationJob job = queue.poll();
                if (job == null) break;
                if (job.isExpired()) continue; // discard stale
                if (idempotencyStore.alreadySent(job.getNotificationId())) continue;
                batch.add(job);
            }
            if (batch.isEmpty()) { sleep(100); continue; }

            rateLimiter.acquire(); // wait for capacity
            firebaseClient.sendBatch(batch); // 1 request -> 500 notifications

            for (NotificationJob job : batch) {
                idempotencyStore.markSent(job.getNotificationId());
            }
        }
    }

    private void sleep(long ms) {
        try { Thread.sleep(ms); } catch (InterruptedException e) { }
    }
}
```

### How does the batch API reduce outbound calls?

Instead of one HTTP call per notification, one call carries 500 device tokens. This dramatically reduces request count and overhead.

```java
package com.bank.notification.provider;

import com.bank.notification.model.NotificationJob;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class FirebaseClient {

    public void sendBatch(List<NotificationJob> batch) {
        List<String> tokens = batch.stream()
            .map(NotificationJob::getDeviceToken)
            .collect(Collectors.toList());

        // Firebase multicast: 1 HTTP request for up to 500 tokens
        // Response includes per-token results: success, invalid, retry
        System.out.printf("Sent batch of %d notifications in 1 request%n",
            tokens.size());
    }
}
```

```text
Sent batch of 500 notifications in 1 request
# Without batching: 500 HTTP calls
# With batching: 1 HTTP call
# For 100M notifications at batch size 500: 200,000 requests instead of 100,000,000
```

### How does idempotency prevent double-sending?

Each notification has a unique ID. Before sending, check if it was already delivered. After sending, record it. This turns at-least-once into effectively exactly-once.

```java
package com.bank.notification.service;

import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;

public class IdempotencyStore {

    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, Boolean> sent = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    public boolean alreadySent(String notificationId) {
        return sent.containsKey(notificationId);
    }

    public void markSent(String notificationId) {
        sent.put(notificationId, true);
    }
}
```

### How do retries and DLQ handle failures?

Transient failures retry with backoff. Permanently failed tokens (invalid, unregistered) go to a dead-letter queue for cleanup.

```java
package com.bank.notification.worker;

public class RetryHandler {

    private static final int MAX_RETRIES = 3;

    public void handleFailure(NotificationJob job, String errorCode, int attempt) {
        if ("INVALID_TOKEN".equals(errorCode) || "UNREGISTERED".equals(errorCode)) {
            // Permanent failure: send to DLQ, clean up token store
            deadLetterQueue.enqueue(job);
            tokenCleanup.removeToken(job.getDeviceToken());
        } else if (attempt < MAX_RETRIES) {
            // Transient: re-enqueue with backoff
            queue.enqueueWithDelay(job, attempt * 5000L);
        } else {
            deadLetterQueue.enqueue(job);
        }
    }
}
```

### How do you track delivery?

The provider reports outcomes asynchronously through callbacks. Update each notification's state by unique ID.

```java
package com.bank.notification.tracking;

public class DeliveryTracker {

    public void onCallback(String notificationId, String status) {
        // status: DELIVERED, OPENED, FAILED
        System.out.printf("Notification %s: %s%n", notificationId, status);
        // Update tracking store, aggregate campaign metrics
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

How do you handle quiet hours and user notification preferences? How do you separate priority streams so OTPs are not stuck behind a marketing blast? How do you monitor queue depth and provider error rate? What happens if the provider rate-limits you despite your token bucket?
